<!-- Section 1: Introduction -->
<section id="sec-intro" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is a Right Triangle Trig Ratios Calculator?</h2>
  <p>A right triangle trig ratios calculator computes the six trigonometric ratios, sine, cosine, tangent, cotangent, secant, and cosecant, from the side lengths of a right triangle. Each ratio compares a specific pair of sides relative to a chosen acute reference angle. The calculator on this page offers six modes, one per ratio, so you enter the two sides each ratio needs and get the value instantly.</p>
  <p>These ratios depend on a reference angle, not just the triangle. For the same triangle, changing which acute angle you measure against swaps the roles of the two legs, so the ratio values change too. That is why the calculator's modes are organized by known side pairs: opposite and hypotenuse give sine, adjacent and hypotenuse give cosine, and the two legs give tangent. The reciprocal modes give cosecant, secant, and cotangent from the same pairs.</p>
  <p>Consider a 3-4-5 triangle with opposite side 3, adjacent side 4, and hypotenuse 5. One triangle produces six different values: <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=3/5=0.6]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=4/5=0.8]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=3/4=0.75]]')} />, with the reciprocal ratios <span set:html={rich('[[\\csc(A)\\approx1.6667]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[\\sec(A)=1.25]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)\\approx1.3333]]')} />. Each ratio asks a different question about the same shape. Understanding the formulas lets you verify any result by hand, which is the surest way to catch mislabeled sides.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 2: Understanding the sides -->
<section id="sec-triangle" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the Sides of a Right Triangle</h2>
  <p>Every trigonometric ratio is a comparison of two sides, so naming the sides correctly is the foundation of everything that follows. This guide uses one consistent convention throughout: <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> is the acute reference angle at the bottom-left, <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} /> is the right angle at the bottom-right, <span set:html={rich('[[C]]')} /> is the other acute angle at the top, side <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> is the vertical leg, side <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> is the horizontal base, and side <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the sloping hypotenuse.</p>

  <h3>What Is a Right Triangle?</h3>
  <p>A right triangle has one angle measuring exactly 90 degrees, with the other two angles acute. The two legs meet at the right angle, and the hypotenuse lies opposite the right angle. Because the three interior angles sum to 180 degrees, the two acute angles are complementary: <span set:html={rich('[[A+C=90^\\circ]]')} />. Right triangles suit trigonometric calculations because fixing one acute angle fixes the triangle's shape completely: all right triangles with the same acute angle are similar, so their side ratios match.</p>

  <h3>What Is the Opposite Side?</h3>
  <p>The opposite side sits directly across from the chosen reference angle. In our convention, side <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> is opposite angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />. In practical problems the opposite side often represents vertical height, an elevation difference, or the vertical component of a slope. Its role is not fixed to a particular side: if the reference angle changes, a different side becomes opposite.</p>

  <h3>What Is the Adjacent Side?</h3>
  <p>The adjacent side touches the reference angle but is not the hypotenuse. In our convention, side <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> is adjacent to angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />. It often represents horizontal run, a ground distance, or a horizontal projection. Of the three sides, the adjacent side is the one most often confused with the opposite side, and that single swap is the most common source of wrong ratio values.</p>

  <h3>What Is the Hypotenuse?</h3>
  <p>The hypotenuse is opposite the right angle and is always the longest side of the triangle. In our convention it is the sloping side <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />. In real situations it can represent a ladder, a cable, a sloping roof support, or a straight-line sight distance. It appears in the sine, cosine, secant, and cosecant formulas, but not directly in the tangent and cotangent formulas, which compare the two legs.</p>

  <h3>Why the Reference Angle Matters</h3>
  <p>Opposite and adjacent are relative labels, not permanent names. For angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />, the opposite side is <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and the adjacent side is <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />. For angle <span set:html={rich('[[C]]')} />, the labels switch: the opposite side is <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> and the adjacent side is <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />. The hypotenuse remains <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> regardless of which acute angle is the reference. Always decide which angle you are measuring against before labeling any side.</p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('trig-ratios-anatomy.svg')} alt="Right triangle with angle A bottom-left highlighted, gold opposite side a, blue adjacent side b, gray hypotenuse c, right angle at B" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl" loading="lazy" />
