  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 3: What are trigonometric ratios -->
<section id="sec-ratios" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding Trigonometric Ratios</h2>
  <p>A trigonometric ratio compares two side lengths of a right triangle relative to a selected acute angle. It describes the triangle's shape, not its size: double every side and all six ratios stay exactly the same, because the numerator and denominator are multiplied by the same factor. A 3-4-5 triangle and a scaled 6-8-10 triangle have identical sine, cosine, tangent, and reciprocal values. This size independence is what makes the ratios useful: they capture angles as pure numbers.</p>

  <h3>Understanding SOH-CAH-TOA</h3>
  <p>SOH-CAH-TOA is the standard memory aid for the three primary ratios. SOH: Sine = Opposite / Hypotenuse. CAH: Cosine = Adjacent / Hypotenuse. TOA: Tangent = Opposite / Adjacent. Read each as a sentence: the sine of an angle is the length of the side opposite that angle divided by the hypotenuse. The aid covers sine, cosine, and tangent; the reciprocal ratios come from a second rule: flip the fraction. Cosecant flips sine, secant flips cosine, and cotangent flips tangent.</p>

  <h3>Why Trigonometric Ratios Are Dimensionless</h3>
  <p>A ratio divides one length by another length, so the units cancel: <span set:html={rich('[[3\\text{ m}/5\\text{ m}=0.6]]')} />. In centimeters the same triangle gives <span set:html={rich('[[300\\text{ cm}/500\\text{ cm}=0.6]]')} />, the identical number. Changing compatible measurement units never changes a trigonometric ratio. The practical consequence is that your inputs must share one unit before you divide, but the result itself carries no unit, which is why the calculator treats all inputs as plain lengths.</p>

  <h3>Why One Triangle Produces Six Ratios</h3>
  <p>Each ratio compares a different pair of sides. Sine compares opposite with hypotenuse, cosine compares adjacent with hypotenuse, and tangent compares opposite with adjacent. Cosecant, secant, and cotangent reverse the corresponding numerator and denominator. Six ratios, three side pairs, two directions per pair. They do not contradict one another; they answer different geometric questions about the same triangle, which is why one calculator offers all six modes.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 4: All six ratios -->
<section id="sec-six" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>The Six Trigonometric Ratios of a Right Triangle</h2>
  <p>This section explains every ratio in the same format: what it means, its formula, which sides it uses, why the fraction is ordered the way it is, a worked example on the 3-4-5 triangle, what the number means, when the ratio is useful, how it relates to another ratio, and one mistake to avoid.</p>

  <h3>1. Sine: Opposite Divided by Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>Sine compares the opposite side with the hypotenuse: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\sin(A)=\\frac{a}{c}}]]]')} />. The hypotenuse is the denominator because it is the longest side, which is what keeps sine between 0 and 1 for acute angles. With <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=3/5]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\sin(A)=0.6}]]]')} />. The result means the opposite side is 60 percent of the hypotenuse. Sine is useful whenever the opposite side and hypotenuse are the known pair, such as finding a ladder's angle from its height and length. It relates to cosine as the complementary partner: the sine of one acute angle equals the cosine of the other. The mistake to avoid: 0.6 is a ratio, not an angle of 0.6 degrees.</p>

  <h3>2. Cosine: Adjacent Divided by Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>Cosine compares the adjacent side with the hypotenuse: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\cos(A)=\\frac{b}{c}}]]]')} />. Both sine and cosine divide by the hypotenuse, so both stay between 0 and 1; they differ only in the numerator, which is why sine and cosine describe different aspects of the same angle. With <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=4/5]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\cos(A)=0.8}]]]')} />. The adjacent side is 80 percent of the hypotenuse. Cosine is useful when the adjacent side and hypotenuse are known, such as a roof's horizontal run and rafter length. It relates to sine through the identity <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin^2(A)+\\cos^2(A)=1]]')} />. The mistake to avoid: swapping the numerators, using 3/5 for cosine instead of 4/5.</p>

  <h3>3. Tangent: Opposite Divided by Adjacent</h3>
  <p>Tangent compares the opposite side with the adjacent side: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\tan(A)=\\frac{a}{b}}]]]')} />. It skips the hypotenuse entirely, which makes it the natural ratio for slope problems where the two legs are the measured quantities. With <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=3/4]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\tan(A)=0.75}]]]')} />. The opposite side is 75 percent as long as the adjacent side. Tangent is the mathematical form of rise over run, so it appears in roof pitch, ramps, and grades. Unlike sine and cosine, tangent can exceed 1 whenever the opposite side is longer than the adjacent side. The mistake to avoid: confusing a tangent of 0.75 with 75 percent of a right angle.</p>

  <h3>4. Cotangent: Adjacent Divided by Opposite</h3>
  <p>Cotangent is the reciprocal of tangent: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\cot(A)=\\frac{b}{a}}]]]')} />. It reverses the side comparison tangent makes, so where tangent asks how steep a slope is, cotangent asks how shallow it is. With <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)=4/3]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\cot(A)\\approx1.3333}]]]')} />. Equivalently, <span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)=1/\\tan(A)=1/0.75\\approx1.3333]]')} />. Cotangent is useful when a problem naturally gives adjacent over opposite, or when a formula is written in reciprocal form. The mistake to avoid: writing cotangent as a/b, which is tangent.</p>

  <h3>5. Secant: Hypotenuse Divided by Adjacent</h3>
  <p>Secant is the reciprocal of cosine: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\sec(A)=\\frac{c}{b}}]]]')} />. Flipping cosine's fraction puts the hypotenuse, the longest side, on top, which is why secant is always greater than 1 for an acute angle in a valid right triangle. With <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[\\sec(A)=5/4]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\sec(A)=1.25}]]]')} />. Equivalently, <span set:html={rich('[[\\sec(A)=1/\\cos(A)=1/0.8=1.25]]')} />. Secant is useful in physics and engineering formulas that are stated with the hypotenuse in the numerator. The mistake to avoid: confusing secant with cosine, or with the inverse cosine function.</p>

  <h3>6. Cosecant: Hypotenuse Divided by Opposite</h3>
  <p>Cosecant is the reciprocal of sine: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\csc(A)=\\frac{c}{a}}]]]')} />. Like secant, it puts the hypotenuse on top, so it is always greater than 1 for acute angles. With <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[\\csc(A)=5/3]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\csc(A)\\approx1.6667}]]]')} />. Equivalently, <span set:html={rich('[[\\csc(A)=1/\\sin(A)=1/0.6\\approx1.6667]]')} />. Cosecant appears in wave and oscillation mathematics where sine's reciprocal is the convenient form. The mistake to avoid: confusing cosecant with inverse sine; <span set:html={rich('[[1/\\sin(A)]]')} /> is cosecant, while <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin^{-1}(r)]]')} /> recovers an angle.</p>

