<!-- Section 5: How to choose -->
<section id="sec-choose" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Choose the Right Trigonometric Ratio</h2>
  <p>The best ratio depends on the two side lengths available in a problem. Each pair of sides allows two reciprocal calculations, and the calculator's six modes match these pairings exactly:</p>
  <div class="not-prose my-6 overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full min-w-[560px] border-collapse text-left text-[15px]">
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-primary/20">
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-headline">Known sides</th>
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-headline">Direct ratio</th>
          <th class="py-3 font-headline">Reciprocal ratio</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr class="border-b border-primary/10"><td class="py-3 pr-4">Opposite and hypotenuse</td><td class="py-3 pr-4">Sine</td><td class="py-3">Cosecant</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-primary/10"><td class="py-3 pr-4">Adjacent and hypotenuse</td><td class="py-3 pr-4">Cosine</td><td class="py-3">Secant</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="py-3 pr-4">Opposite and adjacent</td><td class="py-3 pr-4">Tangent</td><td class="py-3">Cotangent</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>If the opposite side and hypotenuse are known, you can compute <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=a/c]]')} /> and also <span set:html={rich('[[\\csc(A)=c/a]]')} />. If the adjacent side and hypotenuse are known, you can compute <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=b/c]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[\\sec(A)=c/b]]')} />. If the two legs are known, you can compute <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=a/b]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)=b/a]]')} /></p>

  <h3>How to Use the Sine Ratio Mode</h3>
  <p>Select the sine mode when the opposite side and hypotenuse are known. Enter the opposite side in the "Opposite a" field and the hypotenuse in the "Hypotenuse c" field, then click "Calculate sin(A)". No mode requires all three side lengths; each mode asks for exactly the pair its ratio uses.</p>

  <h3>How to Use the Cosine Ratio Mode</h3>
  <p>Select the cosine mode when the adjacent side and hypotenuse are known. Enter the adjacent side in the "Adjacent b" field and the hypotenuse in the "Hypotenuse c" field, then click "Calculate cos(A)".</p>

  <h3>How to Use the Tangent Ratio Mode</h3>
  <p>Select the tangent mode when the two legs are known. Enter the opposite side in the "Opposite a" field and the adjacent side in the "Adjacent b" field, then click "Calculate tan(A)".</p>

  <h3>How to Use the Cotangent Ratio Mode</h3>
  <p>Select the cotangent mode when the legs are known and the problem is stated as adjacent over opposite. Enter the adjacent side first in the "Adjacent b" field and the opposite side in the "Opposite a" field, then click "Calculate cot(A)".</p>

  <h3>How to Use the Secant Ratio Mode</h3>
  <p>Select the secant mode when the hypotenuse and adjacent side are known. Enter the hypotenuse in the "Hypotenuse c" field and the adjacent side in the "Adjacent b" field, then click "Calculate sec(A)".</p>

  <h3>How to Use the Cosecant Ratio Mode</h3>
  <p>Select the cosecant mode when the hypotenuse and opposite side are known. Enter the hypotenuse in the "Hypotenuse c" field and the opposite side in the "Opposite a" field, then click "Calculate csc(A)".</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 6: How to use the calculator -->
<section id="sec-howto" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Use the Right Triangle Trig Ratios Calculator</h2>
  <p>Follow these steps for any of the six modes:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Identify the reference angle.</strong> Decide which acute angle the problem is about. All side labels follow from this choice.</li>
    <li><strong>Label opposite, adjacent, and hypotenuse.</strong> The side across from the reference angle is opposite, the leg touching it is adjacent, and the side across from the right angle is the hypotenuse.</li>
    <li><strong>Determine which two side lengths are known.</strong> Each mode needs exactly one pair.</li>
    <li><strong>Choose the mode matching those two sides.</strong> Use the table in the previous section to pick sine, cosine, tangent, cotangent, secant, or cosecant.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter the first required side length.</strong> The fields are labeled, for example "Opposite a" and "Hypotenuse c".</li>
    <li><strong>Enter the second required side length.</strong> Keep the order the labels show: some modes list the hypotenuse first.</li>
    <li><strong>Select compatible units.</strong> Use the unit selectors beside each input so both measurements share one unit system.</li>
    <li><strong>Check the inputs are positive and valid.</strong> Both legs must be positive and the hypotenuse must be longer than either leg.</li>
    <li><strong>Click the Calculate button.</strong> For example, "Calculate sin(A)" in the sine mode.</li>
    <li><strong>Review the result and solution steps.</strong> The calculator shows the formula, the substitution, and the final value.</li>
    <li><strong>Check the live visualization.</strong> Confirm the triangle diagram labels match your intended reference angle.</li>
    <li><strong>Use Reset for a new calculation.</strong> The Reset button clears all fields so the next calculation starts clean.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Correct side identification matters more than memorizing formulas. Most wrong answers on this page come from a swapped opposite and adjacent, not from arithmetic.</p>

  <h3>Complete Calculator Walkthrough</h3>
  <p>Given opposite side 3, adjacent side 4, and hypotenuse 5, all in the same unit, here is what each mode needs and returns:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Sine mode.</strong> Enter "Opposite a" = 3 and "Hypotenuse c" = 5, click "Calculate sin(A)". Result: 0.6.</li>
    <li><strong>Cosine mode.</strong> Enter "Adjacent b" = 4 and "Hypotenuse c" = 5, click "Calculate cos(A)". Result: 0.8.</li>
    <li><strong>Tangent mode.</strong> Enter "Opposite a" = 3 and "Adjacent b" = 4, click "Calculate tan(A)". Result: 0.75.</li>
    <li><strong>Cotangent mode.</strong> Enter "Adjacent b" = 4 and "Opposite a" = 3, click "Calculate cot(A)". Result: approximately 1.3333.</li>
    <li><strong>Secant mode.</strong> Enter "Hypotenuse c" = 5 and "Adjacent b" = 4, click "Calculate sec(A)". Result: 1.25.</li>
    <li><strong>Cosecant mode.</strong> Enter "Hypotenuse c" = 5 and "Opposite a" = 3, click "Calculate csc(A)". Result: approximately 1.6667.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The ratios are unitless, so the input measurements only need compatible units, not any particular one. The calculator does not recover missing sides or angles from these modes; it computes the requested ratio from the two sides you provide.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 7: Foundational examples -->
<section id="sec-foundations" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Step-by-Step Examples of Trigonometric Ratios</h2>
  <p>These four examples build understanding gradually, from a basic integer triangle to the exact ratios of special triangles.</p>

  <h3>Example 1: A 3-4-5 Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=3\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=4\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5\\text{ cm}]]')} />, calculate all six ratios. Sine: <span set:html={rich('[[3/5=0.6]]')} />. Cosine: <span set:html={rich('[[4/5=0.8]]')} />. Tangent: <span set:html={rich('[[3/4=0.75]]')} />. Cotangent: <span set:html={rich('[[4/3\\approx1.3333]]')} />. Secant: <span set:html={rich('[[5/4=1.25]]')} />. Cosecant: <span set:html={rich('[[5/3\\approx1.6667]]')} />. The ratios differ because each compares a different side pair; they all describe the same angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /></p>

  <h3>Example 2: A 5-12-13 Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=5\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=12\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=13\\text{ m}]]')} />. Sine: <span set:html={rich('[[5/13\\approx0.3846]]')} />. Cosine: <span set:html={rich('[[12/13\\approx0.9231]]')} />. Tangent: <span set:html={rich('[[5/12\\approx0.4167]]')} />. The reciprocals: cosecant <span set:html={rich('[[13/5=2.6]]')} />, secant <span set:html={rich('[[13/12\\approx1.0833]]')} />, cotangent <span set:html={rich('[[12/5=2.4]]')} />. Compared with the 3-4-5 triangle, the longer adjacent side makes cosine larger and tangent smaller, which is what a shallower angle looks like in ratio form.</p>

  <h3>Example 3: A 45-45-90 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=1]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=1]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=\\sqrt{2}]]')} />. Sine and cosine are equal: <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(45^\\circ)=\\cos(45^\\circ)=\\sqrt{2}/2\\approx0.7071]]')} />. Tangent and cotangent both equal 1: <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(45^\\circ)=\\cot(45^\\circ)=1]]')} />. Secant and cosecant are equal: <span set:html={rich('[[\\sec(45^\\circ)=\\csc(45^\\circ)=\\sqrt{2}\\approx1.4142]]')} />. Equal legs make sine equal cosine and tangent equal 1; symmetry between the two acute angles explains all three equalities.</p>

  <h3>Example 4: A 30-60-90 Triangle</h3>
  <p>For angle <span set:html={rich('[[A=30^\\circ]]')} />, use <span set:html={rich('[[a=1]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=\\sqrt{3}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=2]]')} />. Sine: <span set:html={rich('[[1/2=0.5]]')} />. Cosine: <span set:html={rich('[[\\sqrt{3}/2\\approx0.8660]]')} />. Tangent: <span set:html={rich('[[1/\\sqrt{3}\\approx0.5774]]')} />. Cotangent: <span set:html={rich('[[\\sqrt{3}\\approx1.7321]]')} />. Secant: <span set:html={rich('[[2/\\sqrt{3}\\approx1.1547]]')} />. Cosecant: <span set:html={rich('[[2]]')} />. The ratios for 30 and 60 degrees are linked: <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(30^\\circ)=\\cos(60^\\circ)]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(30^\\circ)=\\sin(60^\\circ)]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(30^\\circ)=\\cot(60^\\circ)]]')} />, because the complementary angles swap opposite and adjacent.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 8: Real-life applications -->
<section id="sec-applications" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Real-Life Applications of Trigonometric Ratios</h2>
  <p>Each example uses the ratio that fits the situation, not all six. Every example identifies the triangle model, the known sides, the reference angle, and the meaning of the result, with assumptions stated where they matter.</p>

  <h3>Example 5: Calculating Roof Pitch</h3>
  <p>A roof rises 3 m over a horizontal run of 4 m. The simplified triangle uses rise as the opposite side (a = 3 m) and run as the adjacent side (b = 4 m), with the roof surface as the hypotenuse. Tangent fits because both legs are known: <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=3/4=0.75]]')} />. The percentage grade is <span set:html={rich('[[0.75\\times100\\%=75\\%]]')} />, and the angle is <span set:html={rich('[[A=\\tan^{-1}(0.75)\\approx36.87^\\circ]]')} />. The slope ratio, the percentage grade, and the angle are three ways to say the same thing: the roof climbs 0.75 m for every 1 m of run. This is a geometry example, not a complete roof-design assessment.</p>

  <h3>Example 6: Understanding a Ladder Against a Wall</h3>
  <p>A ladder reaches 4 m up a wall with its base 3 m from the wall. The wall height is the opposite side (a = 4 m), the ground distance is the adjacent side (b = 3 m), and the ladder is the hypotenuse: <span set:html={rich('[[c=\\sqrt{4^2+3^2}=5\\text{ m}]]')} />. For the angle between the ladder and the ground: <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=4/5=0.8]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=3/5=0.6]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=4/3\\approx1.3333]]')} />. The angle is <span set:html={rich('[[A\\approx53.13^\\circ]]')} />. One triangle, one angle, three different ratios, each emphasizing a different side pair. The calculation does not establish ladder safety.</p>

  <h3>Example 7: Evaluating a Ramp's Gradient</h3>
  <p>A ramp rises 0.6 m over a horizontal run of 8 m. Tangent fits the two legs: <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=0.6/8=0.075]]')} />. The percentage grade is <span set:html={rich('[[0.075\\times100\\%=7.5\\%]]')} />, and the angle is <span set:html={rich('[[A=\\tan^{-1}(0.075)\\approx4.29^\\circ]]')} />. A 7.5% grade is not a 7.5-degree angle: percent is the ratio times 100, while degrees measure the angle itself, and they diverge quickly past small values. Accessibility and building-code compliance need additional checks beyond this geometry.</p>

  <h3>Example 8: Measuring a Building's Elevation</h3>
  <p>An observer stands 24 m horizontally from a building, and the top is 18 m above eye level. The adjacent side is 24 m and the opposite side is 18 m, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=18/24=0.75]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[A\\approx36.87^\\circ]]')} />, the angle of elevation. The straight-line sight distance is <span set:html={rich('[[c=\\sqrt{18^2+24^2}=30\\text{ m}]]')} />, giving <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=18/30=0.6]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=24/30=0.8]]')} /> for the same geometry. The result is relative to eye level, not necessarily the total building height.</p>

  <h3>Example 9: Surveying an Elevation Difference</h3>
  <p>A surveyor models a vertical elevation difference of 15 m over a horizontal distance of 20 m. Tangent: <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=15/20=0.75]]')} />. The straight-line distance is <span set:html={rich('[[c=\\sqrt{15^2+20^2}=25\\text{ m}]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=15/25=0.6]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=20/25=0.8]]')} /> describe the same surveyed triangle through different side pairs. Real surveying may need instrument-height adjustments and corrections beyond this flat model.</p>

  <h3>Example 10: Estimating a Tree's Height From Its Shadow</h3>
  <p>A vertical tree casts a 9 m shadow, and the height above the ground is 12 m. With opposite 12 m and adjacent 9 m: <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=12/9\\approx1.3333]]')} />. The line-of-sight hypotenuse of the simplified triangle is <span set:html={rich('[[c=\\sqrt{12^2+9^2}=15\\text{ m}]]')} />, giving <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=12/15=0.8]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=9/15=0.6]]')} />. This assumes level ground, a vertical tree, and a shadow measured to the point directly below the crown.</p>

  <h3>Example 11: Understanding a Guy Wire</h3>
  <p>A guy wire connects to a pole 12 m above the ground, with the anchor 5 m horizontally from the pole. The wire length is <span set:html={rich('[[c=\\sqrt{12^2+5^2}=13\\text{ m}]]')} />. Sine: <span set:html={rich('[[12/13\\approx0.9231]]')} />. Cosine: <span set:html={rich('[[5/13\\approx0.3846]]')} />. Tangent: <span set:html={rich('[[12/5=2.4]]')} />. Tangent exceeds 1 here because the vertical component is longer than the horizontal run; that is normal, not an error. These ratios say nothing about anchor strength or wire tension.</p>

  <h3>Example 12: Understanding Staircase Geometry</h3>
  <p>A simplified staircase has a total rise of 2.4 m and a horizontal run of 3.2 m, with diagonal length <span set:html={rich('[[c=\\sqrt{2.4^2+3.2^2}=4\\text{ m}]]')} />. Sine: <span set:html={rich('[[2.4/4=0.6]]')} />. Cosine: <span set:html={rich('[[3.2/4=0.8]]')} />. Tangent: <span set:html={rich('[[2.4/3.2=0.75]]')} />. The ratios describe the staircase's overall geometry, but they do not establish compliance with staircase regulations, which also constrain individual step dimensions.</p>

  <h3>Example 13: Understanding a Crane Boom's Direction</h3>
  <p>A simplified crane boom has a vertical component of 8 m and a horizontal projection of 6 m, with <span set:html={rich('[[c=\\sqrt{8^2+6^2}=10\\text{ m}]]')} />. Sine <span set:html={rich('[[8/10=0.8]]')} /> describes the boom's vertical share, cosine <span set:html={rich('[[6/10=0.6]]')} /> its horizontal share, and tangent <span set:html={rich('[[8/6\\approx1.3333]]')} /> its steepness. Trigonometric ratios alone do not determine crane capacity or safe operation.</p>

  <h3>Example 14: Estimating a Hiking Trail's Gradient</h3>
  <p>A straight section of trail gains 120 m in elevation over 800 m horizontally. Tangent: <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=120/800=0.15]]')} />. The grade is <span set:html={rich('[[15\\%]]')} /> and the angle is <span set:html={rich('[[A=\\tan^{-1}(0.15)\\approx8.53^\\circ]]')} />. This constant-gradient model does not describe a winding trail, but it gives a useful average steepness for planning effort.</p>

  <h3>Example 15: Checking a Mechanical Brace</h3>
  <p>A diagonal brace has a vertical component of 1.5 m and a horizontal component of 2 m, with <span set:html={rich('[[c=\\sqrt{1.5^2+2^2}=2.5\\text{ m}]]')} />. Sine: <span set:html={rich('[[1.5/2.5=0.6]]')} />. Cosine: <span set:html={rich('[[2/2.5=0.8]]')} />. Tangent: <span set:html={rich('[[1.5/2=0.75]]')} />. These are the 3-4-5 ratios at half scale, showing that proportional scaling leaves every ratio unchanged. This says nothing about the brace's strength or load capacity.</p>

  <h3>Example 16: Understanding a Straight Aircraft Climb</h3>
  <p>A simplified straight flight segment gains 1.5 km vertically over 5 km horizontally. Tangent: <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=1.5/5=0.3]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[A=\\tan^{-1}(0.3)\\approx16.70^\\circ]]')} />. The straight-line distance is <span set:html={rich('[[c=\\sqrt{1.5^2+5^2}\\approx5.22\\text{ km}]]')} />, giving <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)\\approx1.5/5.22\\approx0.287]]')} />. Tangent uses ground distance, sine uses flight-path distance; same climb, different reference lengths. This is a geometry example, not a flight-navigation calculation.</p>

  <h3>Practical Example Comparison</h3>
