<!-- Section 9: Relationships -->
<section id="sec-relate" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How the Six Trigonometric Ratios Relate to One Another</h2>
  <p>The six ratios are not six independent facts. They form three reciprocal pairs, two quotient relationships, and three Pythagorean identities. Knowing these connections turns six formulas into one system, and it gives you verification methods for every calculation.</p>

  <h3>Reciprocal Pairs</h3>
  <p>Each reciprocal pair multiplies to 1 because the fractions cancel: <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)\\csc(A)=1]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)\\sec(A)=1]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)\\cot(A)=1]]')} />. On the 3-4-5 triangle: <span set:html={rich('[[0.6\\times1.6667\\approx1]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[0.8\\times1.25=1]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[0.75\\times1.3333\\approx1]]')} />. Small discrepancies come from rounded decimals, not from the mathematics.</p>

  <h3>Tangent as Sine Divided by Cosine</h3>
  <p>The quotient relationship <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\tan(A)=\\frac{\\sin(A)}{\\cos(A)}}]]]')} /> follows from canceling the shared hypotenuse: <span set:html={rich('[[(a/c)/(b/c)=a/b]]')} />. Check it with the 3-4-5 values: <span set:html={rich('[[0.6/0.8=0.75]]')} />, which matches <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)]]')} />. This is a useful verification: if your tangent does not equal sine divided by cosine, a side is mislabeled.</p>

  <h3>Cotangent as Cosine Divided by Sine</h3>
  <p>Likewise, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\cot(A)=\\frac{\\cos(A)}{\\sin(A)}}]]]')} />. With the 3-4-5 values: <span set:html={rich('[[0.8/0.6\\approx1.3333]]')} />, matching <span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)]]')} />. Cotangent inherits everything tangent knows, flipped.</p>

  <h3>The Pythagorean Trigonometric Identity</h3>
  <p>The identity <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\sin^2(A)+\\cos^2(A)=1}]]]')} /> follows directly from the Pythagorean theorem: <span set:html={rich('[[(a/c)^2+(b/c)^2=(a^2+b^2)/c^2=c^2/c^2=1]]')} />. For the 3-4-5 triangle: <span set:html={rich('[[0.6^2+0.8^2=0.36+0.64=1]]')} />. Two more identities extend the pattern: <span set:html={rich('[[1+\\tan^2(A)=\\sec^2(A)]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[1+\\cot^2(A)=\\csc^2(A)]]')} />, obtained by dividing the first identity by cosine squared and sine squared. They are handy when a problem gives one ratio and needs another without side lengths.</p>

  <h3>How Complementary Angles Affect the Ratios</h3>
  <p>Since <span set:html={rich('[[A+C=90^\\circ]]')} />, switching the reference angle from <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> to <span set:html={rich('[[C]]')} /> swaps opposite and adjacent: <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=\\cos(C)]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=\\sin(C)]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=\\cot(C)]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)=\\tan(C)]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[\\sec(A)=\\csc(C)]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[\\csc(A)=\\sec(C)]]')} />. In the 3-4-5 triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=3/5=0.6]]')} /> while <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(C)=3/5=0.6]]')} />, because the side opposite <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> is adjacent to <span set:html={rich('[[C]]')} />. Every ratio at one acute angle reappears as its cofunction at the other.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 10: Ratios at different angles -->
<section id="sec-angles" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding Trigonometric Ratios at Different Angles</h2>
  <p>As an acute reference angle grows, the ratios change in predictable ways. Sine increases from near 0 toward 1, while cosine decreases from near 1 toward 0. Tangent increases from near 0 without an upper limit, while cotangent decreases from large values toward 0. Secant increases from near 1 without limit, while cosecant decreases from large values toward 1. These are limiting behaviors near 0 and 90 degrees, not values an acute angle in a valid non-degenerate right triangle actually attains at the endpoints.</p>
  <div class="not-prose my-6 overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full min-w-[480px] border-collapse text-left text-[15px]">
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-primary/20">
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-headline">Angle</th>
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-headline">Sine</th>
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-headline">Cosine</th>
          <th class="py-3 font-headline">Tangent</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr class="border-b border-primary/10"><td class="py-3 pr-4"><span set:html={rich('[[30^\\circ]]')} /></td><td class="py-3 pr-4">0.5</td><td class="py-3 pr-4">Approximately 0.8660</td><td class="py-3">Approximately 0.5774</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-primary/10"><td class="py-3 pr-4"><span set:html={rich('[[45^\\circ]]')} /></td><td class="py-3 pr-4">Approximately 0.7071</td><td class="py-3 pr-4">Approximately 0.7071</td><td class="py-3">1</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="py-3 pr-4"><span set:html={rich('[[60^\\circ]]')} /></td><td class="py-3 pr-4">Approximately 0.8660</td><td class="py-3 pr-4">0.5</td><td class="py-3">Approximately 1.7321</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>The pattern is symmetric: sine at 30 degrees equals cosine at 60 degrees, reflecting the complementary relationship. Cotangent, secant, and cosecant come from the reciprocal relationships: for example, at 30 degrees, <span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(30^\\circ)=1/0.5774\\approx1.7321]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[\\csc(30^\\circ)=1/0.5=2]]')} /></p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('trig-ratios-flowchart.svg')} alt="Flowchart: pick sine or cosecant for opposite and hypotenuse, cosine or secant for adjacent and hypotenuse, tangent or cotangent for the two legs" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Choose your calculator mode from the side pair you know: each pair gives a direct ratio and its reciprocal.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 11: Missing side -->
<section id="sec-missing-side" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Calculate a Missing Side of a Right Triangle</h2>
  <p>The six-ratio calculator finds ratios from known sides. Finding an unknown side from a known angle and side is a related but different task, solved by rearranging the ratio formulas. These are companion calculations, not features of the ratio calculator itself.</p>

  <h3>Finding the Opposite Side With Sine</h3>
  <p>Start from <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=a/c]]')} /> and rearrange: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{a=c\\sin(A)}]]]')} />. With hypotenuse 10 m and angle <span set:html={rich('[[A=30^\\circ]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[a=10\\sin(30^\\circ)=5\\text{ m}]]')} /></p>

  <h3>Finding the Adjacent Side With Cosine</h3>
  <p>Start from <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=b/c]]')} /> and rearrange: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{b=c\\cos(A)}]]]')} />. With hypotenuse 10 m and angle <span set:html={rich('[[A=60^\\circ]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[b=10\\cos(60^\\circ)=5\\text{ m}]]')} /></p>

  <h3>Finding the Opposite Side With Tangent</h3>
  <p>Start from <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=a/b]]')} /> and rearrange: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{a=b\\tan(A)}]]]')} />. With adjacent side 8 m and angle <span set:html={rich('[[A=30^\\circ]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[a=8\\tan(30^\\circ)\\approx4.62\\text{ m}]]')} />. Tangent is especially useful here because it connects the two legs directly, with no hypotenuse involved.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 12: Inverse trig -->
<section id="sec-inverse" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Find an Angle From a Trigonometric Ratio</h2>
  <p>A trigonometric ratio is not an angle. When the ratio is known and the angle is unknown, inverse trigonometric functions recover the acute reference angle: <span set:html={rich('[[A=\\sin^{-1}(r)]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[A=\\cos^{-1}(r)]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[A=\\tan^{-1}(r)]]')} />. These are called inverse sine, inverse cosine, and inverse tangent, also known as arcsine, arccosine, and arctangent.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example Using Sine</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=0.6]]')} />, the angle is <span set:html={rich('[[A=\\sin^{-1}(0.6)\\approx36.87^\\circ]]')} /></p>

  <h3>Worked Example Using Cosine</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=0.8]]')} />, the angle is <span set:html={rich('[[A=\\cos^{-1}(0.8)\\approx36.87^\\circ]]')} /></p>

  <h3>Worked Example Using Tangent</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=0.75]]')} />, the angle is <span set:html={rich('[[A=\\tan^{-1}(0.75)\\approx36.87^\\circ]]')} /></p>

  <p>All three recover the same angle because all three ratios describe the same 3-4-5 triangle. The difference between an inverse function and a reciprocal matters here: <span set:html={rich('[[1/\\sin(A)=\\csc(A)]]')} /> is a reciprocal, a different ratio, while <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin^{-1}(r)]]')} /> is an inverse function, which returns an angle. When computing angles by hand, check whether your tool is in degree or radian mode; this page's calculator works in ratios only, so angle recovery is a separate step.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 13: Mistakes -->
<section id="sec-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Using Trigonometric Ratios</h2>

  <h3>Confusing the Opposite Side With the Adjacent Side</h3>
  <p>The labels depend on the reference angle. In a 3-4-5 triangle, computing <span set:html={rich('[[4/5=0.8]]')} /> for sine actually gives cosine. Fix: mark the reference angle first, then label the side across from it as opposite.</p>

  <h3>Identifying Sides Relative to the Wrong Reference Angle</h3>
  <p>Labeling for angle C while the problem asks about angle A swaps every ratio. Fix: write down which angle is the reference before touching the calculator.</p>

  <h3>Using Sine Instead of Cosine</h3>
  <p>Sine needs the opposite side; cosine needs the adjacent side. If you know the adjacent side and hypotenuse but compute <span set:html={rich('[[a/c]]')} /> anyway, you have swapped the legs. Fix: match the ratio to the known pair using the table in the choosing section.</p>

  <h3>Using Tangent When the Known Sides Are Opposite and Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>Tangent needs the two legs. With opposite and hypotenuse known, the correct direct ratio is sine. Fix: check which pair you actually have before choosing the mode.</p>

  <h3>Reversing the Numerator and Denominator</h3>
  <p>Computing <span set:html={rich('[[c/a]]')} /> when you meant sine gives cosecant, a value above 1 instead of below 1. Fix: the denominator is the hypotenuse for sine and cosine, the adjacent leg for tangent.</p>

  <h3>Confusing Tangent With Cotangent</h3>
  <p>Tangent is <span set:html={rich('[[a/b]]')} />; cotangent is <span set:html={rich('[[b/a]]')} />. They are reciprocals, so one error inverts the answer. Fix: read the mode label before entering values.</p>

  <h3>Confusing Cosine With Secant</h3>
  <p>Cosine is <span set:html={rich('[[b/c]]')} />, always below 1; secant is <span set:html={rich('[[c/b]]')} />, always above 1. If your secant comes out below 1, you computed cosine. Fix: reciprocal ratios put the hypotenuse on top.</p>

  <h3>Confusing Sine With Cosecant</h3>
  <p>The same inversion: sine is <span set:html={rich('[[a/c]]')} />, cosecant is <span set:html={rich('[[c/a]]')} />. Fix: check whether your result sits in the expected range for the mode you chose.</p>

  <h3>Assuming Every Ratio Must Be Less Than 1</h3>
  <p>Sine and cosine are below 1, but tangent, cotangent, secant, and cosecant can all exceed 1. A tangent of 2.4 is perfectly valid for a steep slope. Fix: learn the valid range of each ratio.</p>

  <h3>Assuming a Ratio Such as 0.6 Represents an Angle of 0.6 Degrees</h3>
  <p>A ratio is a pure number, not a degree measure. The angle behind <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=0.6]]')} /> is about 36.87 degrees, found with inverse sine. Fix: ratios answer "how do the sides compare"; inverse functions answer "what is the angle".</p>

  <h3>Forgetting That Ratios Are Dimensionless</h3>
  <p>Entering opposite in inches and hypotenuse in feet corrupts the ratio, because the units no longer cancel. Fix: convert both inputs to one unit first.</p>

  <h3>Mixing Incompatible Measurement Units</h3>
  <p>Same root cause, worth repeating: a ratio of 3 inches to 5 centimeters is not 0.6. Fix: use the unit selectors on both inputs before calculating.</p>

  <h3>Entering Zero or Negative Side Lengths</h3>
  <p>A triangle side must be positive. Zero or negative inputs describe no triangle, and the calculator cannot produce a meaningful ratio. Fix: re-measure or re-read the problem.</p>

  <h3>Using Side Lengths That Do Not Form a Valid Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>If the sides fail the Pythagorean check, for example 2, 3, and 10, no right triangle exists with those lengths. Fix: verify <span set:html={rich('[[a^2+b^2=c^2]]')} /> before computing ratios.</p>

  <h3>Rounding Too Early</h3>
  <p>Rounding an intermediate ratio to one decimal place then multiplying magnifies the error. Fix: keep full precision through every step and round only the final answer.</p>

  <h3>Confusing Reciprocal Ratios With Inverse Trigonometric Functions</h3>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\csc(A)=1/\\sin(A)]]')} /> is a ratio; <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin^{-1}(0.6)]]')} /> is an angle. The similar notation causes real confusion. Fix: reciprocals flip fractions, inverse functions recover angles.</p>

  <h3>Treating the Hypotenuse as One of the Legs</h3>
  <p>The hypotenuse is always the longest side and always opposite the right angle. Assigning it as a leg breaks every ratio. Fix: find the longest side first and label it c.</p>

  <h3>Assuming the Six Ratios Have the Same Value</h3>
  <p>In a 3-4-5 triangle the six values run from 0.6 to 1.6667. Only special triangles like 45-45-90 make some ratios equal. Fix: expect six different numbers from one triangle.</p>

  <h3>Assuming the Same Side Is Always Opposite</h3>
  <p>The side opposite angle A is adjacent to angle C. Every worked answer must state its reference angle. Fix: relabel sides whenever the reference angle changes.</p>

  <h3>Treating a Simplified Result as Engineering Proof</h3>
  <p>A ratio check on a brace or a guy wire describes geometry, not strength, tension, or code compliance. Fix: use ratios for the geometry step, and qualified engineering analysis for safety decisions.</p>

  <h3>Wrong Versus Correct: A Concrete Example</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} />, an incorrect sine calculation uses the wrong side pair: <span set:html={rich('[[3/4=0.75]]')} />. That is the tangent ratio, not sine. The correct calculation is <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=3/5=0.6]]')} />. Verify with the quotient relationship: <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=\\sin(A)/\\cos(A)=0.6/0.8=0.75]]')} />. Identifying the correct sides is essential, because the arithmetic is trivial once the sides are right.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 14: Verify -->
<section id="sec-verify" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Check Whether Your Trigonometric Results Are Valid</h2>
  <p>A valid, non-degenerate right triangle requires positive legs, a hypotenuse longer than either leg, sides satisfying the Pythagorean theorem, compatible units, the correct side pair for the chosen ratio, and reciprocal pairs that agree. Four quick methods cover all of this.</p>

  <h3>Verification Method 1: Check the Side Lengths</h3>
  <p>For the 3-4-5 triangle: <span set:html={rich('[[3^2+4^2=5^2]]')} />, or <span set:html={rich('[[9+16=25]]')} />. Passing the Pythagorean check confirms the sides describe a real right triangle before you trust any ratio derived from them.</p>

  <h3>Verification Method 2: Check Reciprocal Pairs</h3>
  <p>Each direct-reciprocal pair must multiply to 1: <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)\\csc(A)=0.6\\times1.6667\\approx1]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)\\sec(A)=0.8\\times1.25=1]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)\\cot(A)=0.75\\times1.3333\\approx1]]')} />. Small differences come from rounded decimals.</p>

  <h3>Verification Method 3: Check Tangent Using Sine and Cosine</h3>
  <p>The quotient relationship gives an independent check: <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=\\sin(A)/\\cos(A)=0.6/0.8=0.75]]')} />. If the calculator's tangent mode and this computation disagree, a side was mislabeled.</p>

  <h3>Verification Method 4: Check the Inverse Angle</h3>
  <p>Recover the angle and see whether it is sensible: <span set:html={rich('[[A=\\tan^{-1}(0.75)\\approx36.87^\\circ]]')} />. An acute result between 0 and 90 degrees that matches the triangle's shape confirms the ratios.</p>
</section>

